;; ===========================================================================
;; Public-API manifest — CURATED metadata sidecar.
;;
;; This file is the human-curated half of the public-API manifest's
;; single-source design. The generator
;; (implementation/scripts/api-manifest/) derives EXISTENCE + :kind +
;; :facade? from live public vars; it joins those against the
;; :classification map here for the three human-classification axes that
;; cannot be derived from code:
;;
;;   :tier   — the closed Tier vocabulary (spec/API.md §Tier taxonomy):
;;             :front-porch | :advanced | :tooling | :adapter | :testing
;;             | :internal-public | :implementation | :deprecated.
;;             Restrictive-by-default: when ambiguous, pick the MORE
;;             restrictive tier. NOTE: `:internal-public` means
;;             a SUPPORTED host/tool embedding point (the Xray `mount-<panel>!`
;;             family); `:implementation` means public-for-technical-reasons
;;             only (a cross-namespace / test reach that is NOT a supported
;;             surface — an app or tool MUST NOT depend on it).
;;   :owner  — the canonical owning Spec doc (e.g. "002", "Tool-Pair",
;;             "009", "007") — the "where does the contract live" anchor.
;;   :status — shipping lineage (spec/API.md §Conventions Status).
;;
;; ...plus TWO MORE on `:facade? true` rows only — the FACADE-AUDIT axes.
;; spec/Conventions.md §Facade policy makes a diff that adds a
;; public var to a facade record FOUR fields in the same PR: tier, owner
;; spec, facade-placement justification, and recommended action. Fields 1
;; and 2 are `:tier` / `:owner` above; fields 3 and 4 are:
;;
;;   :justification — WHY this var is on the facade. For a `:front-porch`
;;                    row, what makes it day-one vocabulary; for anything
;;                    else, why a deliberate single-import re-export beats a
;;                    subnamespace door (Conventions §Keep product facades
;;                    coherent). Conventions is explicit that "it was
;;                    convenient" is not a justification and "every consumer
;;                    calls it on their first hour" is. A blank string is
;;                    refused exactly as a missing one.
;;   :action        — the placement verdict, from the CLOSED vocabulary
;;                    :keep | :rename | :move | :internal-public.
;;                    `:keep` = front-porch, or a justified single-import
;;                    re-export. `:rename` = a lifecycle-verb-law or
;;                    vocabulary violation (the surface stays, the name
;;                    changes). `:move` = it belongs behind an existing
;;                    subnamespace door. `:internal-public` = a host/tool
;;                    embed seam, not a consumer surface.
;;                    `:move` is deliberately RECORDABLE on a live facade
;;                    row: the table must be able to say "this is ruled to
;;                    move" before the move lands. The gate that makes it
;;                    happen is the tracker edge onto the release bead, not
;;                    this file.
;;
;; The generator REFUSES any `:facade? true` row missing `:justification` or
;; carrying an `:action` outside that set, so the diff-time obligation
;; has a mechanical home rather than living only in PR bodies. Both axes are
;; scoped to facade rows: Conventions puts the obligation on FACADE exports,
;; and requiring prose on all ~528 rows would be a different, larger rule.
;;
;; HAND-EDIT THIS FILE; never hand-edit spec/api-manifest.edn (generated).
;; Adding a public var? Add its [namespace var] entry here, then
;; regenerate. The CI drift-check (lint.yml → api-manifest job) goes red
;; until both this sidecar and the generated manifest are updated.
;;
;; The :classification map is keyed by ["namespace" "var"] (both strings).
;; ===========================================================================
{:meta
 {:doc      "Curated tier/owner/status sidecar for the public-API manifest."
  :keystone "rf2-3nbl5.2"}

 ;; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; CLJS-ONLY surfaces — public vars in ClojureScript-only namespaces that
 ;; cannot be `require`d on the JVM, so the generator carries them verbatim.
 ;; Each row is the FULL manifest row, and carries its own
 ;; `:runtime-verified?` flag.
 ;;
 ;; RUNTIME VERIFICATION. The CLJS-side enumeration probe
 ;; (implementation/scripts/api-manifest/probe/, run by `npm run test:cljs`)
 ;; reconciles the LIVE public vars of each covered namespace against these
 ;; rows — the CLJS equivalent of the JVM `ns-publics` existence-check. A
 ;; row whose namespace the probe covers is `:runtime-verified? true`:
 ;;   - the two adapter namespaces (Reagent / UIx) are
 ;;     fully-rowed (their public surface IS the documented adapter API per
 ;;     spec/API.md §UIx adapter), so a var added / removed / renamed
 ;;     there turns the probe RED;
 ;;   - the Xray `mounted?` read is a curated row off the `internal-public`
 ;;     mount surface (the host-embed machinery is "otherwise unrowed-
 ;;     internal" per §Tiering of cross-tool surfaces) — the probe verifies
 ;;     it still resolves to a live var (removed / renamed → RED).
 ;; The `re-frame.core` `frame-provider` reader-conditional row is owned by
 ;; the JVM generator's view of re-frame.core, not this probe, so it stays
 ;; `:runtime-verified? false`. The pair-MCP server, a CLJS-only surface,
 ;; compiles under its own shadow-cljs build and carries no rows here; a
 ;; probe for it would ride that build.
 ;; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 :cljs-only
 [;; --- re-frame.core CLJS-only front-porch surface ---
  ;; `frame-provider` is a Reagent component re-exported from re-frame.core
  ;; under a `#?(:cljs ...)` reader-conditional, so it is absent from the
  ;; JVM `ns-publics` of re-frame.core. Rowed front-porch in spec/API.md
  ;; §View ergonomics. Not covered by the CLJS probe (which loads the
  ;; adapter / Xray namespaces, not re-frame.core's CLJS branch).
  {:namespace "re-frame.core" :var "frame-provider" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :front-porch :owner "002" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? false
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front-porch: the CLJS component that binds a frame for a whole subtree so views beneath it never spell the {:frame f} override. API.md View ergonomics rows it front-porch."}
  ;; `frame-root` is the ENSURE sibling of `frame-provider`:
  ;; a Reagent component re-exported from re-frame.core under `#?(:cljs ...)`,
  ;; absent from the JVM `ns-publics`. Rowed front-porch in spec/API.md
  ;; §View ergonomics. Not covered by the CLJS probe (loads adapters, not
  ;; re-frame.core's CLJS branch).
  {:namespace "re-frame.core" :var "frame-root" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :front-porch :owner "002" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? false
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front-porch: the ENSURE sibling of frame-provider -- the mount recipe an app's root uses. Same View ergonomics home, same tier."}
  ;; --- Reagent adapter (day8/re-frame2-reagent) — probe fully-rowed ---
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.reagent" :var "adapter"             :kind :var :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.reagent" :var "flush-views!"        :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.reagent" :var "set-hiccup-emitter!" :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  ;; The reusable client root: allocate inert, render (create or
  ;; hydrate once, then update the same Root), unmount idempotently. Backed by
  ;; the adapter's active-root ownership (Spec 006 §`render` / §Adapter
  ;; disposal lifecycle). The published slim artefact renames its adapter ns
  ;; to this one at publication, so it publishes the same trio here.
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.reagent" :var "client-root"         :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.reagent" :var "render!"             :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.reagent" :var "unmount!"            :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  ;; --- UIx adapter (day8/re-frame2-uix) — probe fully-rowed ---
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "adapter"             :kind :var :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "use-sub"             :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  ;; capture-frame in hook position: returns EXACTLY the
  ;; capture-frame ops map for the ambient provider frame. UIx only
  ;; (Reagent's spelling of the same hold primitive is reg-view injection).
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "use-frame"           :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "frame-provider"      :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "frame-root"          :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "flush-views!"        :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "set-hiccup-emitter!" :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  ;; The same reusable client root the ratom adapters publish, on every
  ;; React view adapter: allocate inert, render
  ;; (create or hydrate once, then update the same Root), unmount
  ;; idempotently. The tree is a React ELEMENT here (`uix.core/$`) rather
  ;; than hiccup; everything else is the Reagent contract verbatim, and both
  ;; ride the shared spine factory so they cannot drift.
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "client-root"         :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "render!"             :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  {:namespace "re-frame.adapter.uix" :var "unmount!"            :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :adapter :owner "006" :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
  ;; --- Xray (day8/re-frame2-xray) — the live public surface.
  ;;     Real :cljs-only rows, so the Xray-spec projection check resolves them
  ;;     to manifest rows and the CLJS probe runtime-verifies them.
  ;;     The mount family is
  ;;     `internal-public` per spec/API.md §Tiering of cross-tool surfaces (the
  ;;     standing classification); the published constants + the Xray↔MCP read
  ;;     seam + the editor-chip are `tooling` (dev/inspection surfaces consumed
  ;;     by story-mode chrome, docs generators, and the pair-MCP servers — not
  ;;     application logic). Owner "tools/xray"; status "post-v1 lib".
  ;;
  ;;     TWO PROBE POSTURES, and the split is deliberate. The
  ;;     `core` FACADE below is FULLY-ROWED — the probe checks both
  ;;     directions, so an export added there without a row, or a row with no
  ;;     live var, goes RED. Every OTHER Xray namespace here stays a curated
  ;;     SUBSET (direction-1 only — the probe verifies each rowed var still
  ;;     resolves; rename / removal → RED, but an unrowed addition is
  ;;     tolerated). The facade earns the stricter posture because it is the
  ;;     one namespace whose whole point is to BE the enumerated surface:
  ;;     tools/xray/spec/API.md §Wider public surface rows all sixteen of its
  ;;     exports by name, so a seventeenth arriving unclassified is exactly
  ;;     the accretion Conventions §Facade policy exists to stop. The subset
  ;;     namespaces are ordinary implementation namespaces that happen to
  ;;     publish a few documented vars; holding them to completeness would
  ;;     row their internals. ---
  ;;
  ;; day8.re-frame2-xray.core — THE FACADE. spec/Conventions.md §Facade
  ;; policy names three facade namespaces and this is the third; these rows
  ;; are what let the generator's facade-audit invariants reach it.
  ;;
  ;; `:facade? true` is carried PER ROW here rather than derived from
  ;; `gen/facade-namespaces`, because `facade?` is a JVM-side predicate and
  ;; this namespace is CLJS-only — see that Var's docstring for the two
  ;; routes. The roster still names it, so the non-vacuity test
  ;; `live-manifest-has-facade-rows-in-every-enrolled-facade` guards these
  ;; sixteen flags: blank them and it goes RED.
  ;;
  ;; `:tier :tooling` on all sixteen, per Conventions §Story / Xray nuance —
  ;; for a tooling PRODUCT, tooling can legitimately BE the front porch, and
  ;; the discriminator is the user's workflow, not the tier label. Story reads
  ;; the same way on its own evidence: the two products are alike in the one
  ;; respect that matters here
  ;; (a single tooling artefact with no late-bind seam), and Xray's own spec
  ;; supplies the evidence per row below. `:implementation` is refused for
  ;; facade rows outright (gen/implementation-facade-rows), and the eligible
  ;; doc-coverage tiers (`:front-porch` / `:advanced` / `:adapter` /
  ;; `:testing`) would oblige a docs/api/ page per var, which Xray documents
  ;; in its own spec tree instead.
  ;;
  ;; ALL SIXTEEN ARE `:action :keep`, and that is a measured result rather
  ;; than a default. Xray's own spec performs the move analysis:
  ;; tools/xray/spec/API.md §Wider
  ;; public surface names the TWO surfaces deliberately NOT re-exported
  ;; through the facade (the per-key config setters beyond the four
  ;; highest-traffic ones, and the keybinding attach!/detach! lifecycle pair)
  ;; and says why for each. The line between porch and workshop is drawn and
  ;; documented; every var below sits on the porch side of a line the product
  ;; spec argues. Nothing here is a move candidate under the audit
  ;; discriminator (an existing owning namespace is already a clearer door
  ;; AND ordinary hosts do not need the name) — each name backs a step of an
  ;; ordinary host workflow: install, control visibility, pick the inspected
  ;; frame, focus a panel, configure at boot.
  ;;
  ;; NO RENAME IS RECORDED. The spelling is consistent: the thirteen
  ;; mutators all carry a bang, and the three no-bang names (`status`,
  ;; `target-frame`, `valid-focus-panels`) are reads. `load-theme!` mutates
  ;; (it installs a <style> override into <head> via
  ;; global-styles/set-host-theme-css!, which DOES carry the bang), so it
  ;; carries the bang under §Naming bucket 3 even though `load-*` is on NO
  ;; row of the closed lifecycle-verb roster: the roster names WHICH
  ;; lifecycle verb a surface takes, §Naming decides WHETHER it carries `!`,
  ;; and a verb missing from the roster is not a reason to drop the bang.
  ;; That is NOT a licence to coin new lifecycle verbs — Conventions
  ;; §Lifecycle-verb law makes adding a verb to that table a Spec change
  ;; rather than a per-author call.
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "init!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch for a tooling product: the manual-install entry point, and one of exactly two install paths a host chooses between (the other is wiring day8.re-frame2-xray.preload into :devtools/preloads). tools/xray/spec/API.md §Canonical documents it first. Its role against configure! and persisted Settings is fixed: configure! is static boot config, init! the lifecycle hook that receives the merged config. A host that requires the facade at all requires it for this."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "open!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch: the default true-inline mount, and by tools/xray/spec/API.md §open! / open-overlay! / popout! what 95% of host code reaches for. Conventions §Lifecycle-verb law rows open! / close! as the visibility pair, so the spelling is the law's own. Also one of the six verbs the preload mirrors onto window.day8.re_frame2_xray, which keeps the CLJS and JS-console vocabularies identical."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "open-overlay!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Names a DISTINCT mount surface, not a mode of open!: the transient ESC-closeable overlay that floats above the host instead of participating in its layout. A mode-symmetric triplet (open-inline! / open-overlay! / open-popout!) is REFUSED because the three verbs name kinds of mount, and flattening them would cost the rank signal that bare open! is the default. That is why no :rename is recorded here."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "close!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch: the visibility half of the open! / close! pair Conventions §Lifecycle-verb law names -- close! hides an already-mounted shell and leaves it re-open!-able, which is why it is NOT unmount! or destroy-*. Splitting it from open! would split one two-call workflow across two requires."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "toggle!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch: the verb behind Xray's own Ctrl+Shift+C chord, so a host that takes the chord back via keybinding/detach! rebinds it to exactly this var. Documented in tools/xray/spec/API.md §Canonical and mirrored onto the browser global with the other five mount verbs."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "popout!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Names a DISTINCT mount surface: the same-browser second window, where the shell gets its own document context, React root, theme cascade and keybinding. Covered by the same kinds-of-mount reasoning as open-overlay!, and by 017-Test-Coverage-Matrix as a shipped host-facing launch mode."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "status" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch, and the FIRST call a host makes when Xray does not appear: it returns the mount/API state including the last non-blocking diagnostic for a missing [data-rf-xray-host] layout host. tools/xray/spec/API.md §Wider public surface rows it among the six browser globals the preload installs, so the console recovery path and the CLJS one are the same var. A read, hence no bang."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "target-frame" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch: the one-shot read of which HOST frame Xray is inspecting -- the read half of the frame-picker pair, returning nil for UNSELECTED because there is no :rf/default absence-repair fallback (EP-0002). Reactive consumers subscribe to :rf.xray/target-frame directly, so this var exists precisely for the non-reactive host call the facade is for."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "set-target-frame!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch: the write half of the pair above, and the surface a host with several frames needs on its first afternoon. Named parallel to the :rf.xray/target-frame sub and :rf.xray/set-target-frame event it drives, so the facade spelling matches runtime reality. Banged per Conventions §Naming bucket 3 -- it mutates Xray's own :rf/xray frame."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "focus!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "An explicitly-recorded deliberate single-import re-export, which is the exception Conventions §Facade policy provides for: tools/xray/spec/API.md §Wider public surface rows it in the focus namespace as `focus! (the entry point, re-exported through core)`. The owning namespace day8.re-frame2-xray.focus keeps the command shape, the ownership boundary and the pure translation fn; the facade carries only the host-facing door, because the host sending the command is Story, not Xray. Contract at 008-Embedding-Contract §Host-facing focus API."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "valid-focus-panels" :kind :var :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Kept WITH focus! because it is step one of that same two-call workflow: a host validates a panel selector against this set BEFORE sending a focus command, so moving it behind the focus namespace would split a two-line host snippet across two requires for no gain. Spelled differently on the facade from the owner's valid-panels to say WHICH panels -- the facade has no other panel vocabulary, so the bare name would read as the whole panel inventory. It mirrors the live Dynamic L4 tab registry, so it tracks shipped tabs rather than pinning a list."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "load-theme!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "PLACEMENT is what this axis records: tools/xray/spec/API.md §Canonical rows it, and editor-driven palette sync is an ordinary host workflow reached in one call, so it stays on the facade. It mutates -- it installs a <style> override into <head> through global-styles/set-host-theme-css!, which does carry the bang -- so Conventions §Naming bucket 3 bangs it: load-theme!. There is NO `load-*` row in the closed lifecycle-verb roster: the roster names WHICH lifecycle verb a surface takes, §Naming decides WHETHER it carries !, and a verb missing from the roster is not a reason to drop the bang -- the same reading that spells register-listener! (spec/API.md) without a roster row. Adding a bang to an existing verb coins no new operation family, and this is not a licence to invent lifecycle verbs."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "configure!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch, and named by Conventions §Lifecycle-verb law on THIS facade specifically -- configure! is the config-mutation verb across re-frame.core, re-frame.story and day8.re-frame2-xray.core alike. It is also the entry point the boot order is written around: requiring the facade is side-effect-free, so a host calls configure! and only then init!, which is what lets :rf.xray/auto-open? false win deterministically before any auto-open."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "set-auto-open!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "One of exactly four per-key setters tools/xray/spec/API.md §Wider public surface names as deliberately re-exported so common boot wiring lands in one require -- and the same section names the setters deliberately NOT re-exported (set-project-root!, set-keybinding-enabled!, set-filter-seed! and the rest, which hosts reach through day8.re-frame2-xray.config). The porch/workshop line this audit exists to draw is therefore already drawn and documented for this family, and this var is on the porch side of it: suppressing auto-open is boot-time wiring almost every embedding host does."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "set-editor!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "One of the four highest-traffic setters §Wider public surface re-exports by name. It backs the Open-in-editor affordance, which is per-developer configuration nearly every host sets once at boot, and its accepted values (:vscode / :cursor / :windsurf / :zed / :idea / {:custom <uri-template>}) are the ones a reader most often wants beside init!."}
  {:namespace "day8.re-frame2-xray.core" :var "set-egress-profile!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "tools/xray" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "One of the four highest-traffic setters §Wider public surface re-exports by name, and the one with the strongest reason to be legible from the facade: it selects the EP-0015 per-(tool,frame) reveal grain, where :rf.egress/local-redacted is the default and :rf.egress/local-raw is the trusted-local operator opt-in that reveals sensitive AND large values verbatim. A privacy opt-in belongs where a reviewer will see it, not behind a subnamespace door."}
