  ;; ---- re-frame.story — the CLJS ARM of a SPLIT-HOST FACADE ----
  ;;
  ;; `re-frame.story` is a split-host `.cljc` exactly like
  ;; `re-frame.fresco` below: the JVM `ns-publics` sees its JVM-visible
  ;; exports (rowed under `:classification`), and its `#?(:cljs …)` arm
  ;; publishes five more facade fns the JVM cannot see. They are rowed here
  ;; rather than silenced on the `:story-spec-known-unmanifested` allowlist,
  ;; so the facade-audit invariants reach them.
  ;;
  ;; `:facade? true` is carried PER ROW, the same second route the Xray block
  ;; above uses and for a related but not identical reason: `gen/facade?`
  ;; DOES name `re-frame.story`, but it is consulted only on the
  ;; JVM-introspected path (`build-rows`), so a CLJS-arm row never reaches
  ;; it. Blank these five flags and the facade-audit invariants stop seeing
  ;; the five vars while `--check` stays green.
  ;;
  ;; `:tier :tooling` and `:owner "007"` throughout, matching the JVM-visible
  ;; rows these five belong beside: Story is a tooling PRODUCT, so per
  ;; spec/Conventions.md §Story / Xray nuance tooling legitimately IS its
  ;; front porch and the discriminator is the user's workflow.
  ;;
  ;; ALL FIVE ARE `:action :keep`: the move discriminator asks
  ;; whether an existing owning namespace is already a clearer door AND
  ;; ordinary users do not need the name. The doors exist
  ;; (`re-frame.story.ui.multi-substrate`, `re-frame.story.ui.shell`) but the
  ;; second half fails on every one of the five — each is called by ordinary
  ;; HOST BOOT code, through the facade, in this repo's own testbeds and
  ;; docs. Moving them would also split two pairs (register/query,
  ;; mount/unmount+read) across a door boundary, which is the incoherence
  ;; §Keep product facades coherent forbids.
  ;;
  ;; `:runtime-verified? true` on all five is backed by the probe, not a
  ;; hand-set boolean: the CLJS enumeration probe `:require`s
  ;; `re-frame.story` and holds it `:fully-rowed`, so a rename or removal of
  ;; any of the five turns `npm run test:cljs` RED, and
  ;; `runtime-verified-rows-are-actually-covered` fails if this flag is ever
  ;; true without that coverage.
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "register-substrate!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "The host-boot verb of the multi-substrate feature: a host calls it once per substrate it wants Story to render against, which is ordinary consumer workflow rather than chrome-internal plumbing. tools/story/spec/002-Runtime.md §Substrate hooks and 005-SOTA-Features.md §Multi-substrate side-by-side rendering are its normative home; the fresco_counter testbed calls it through the facade at boot; story_multi_substrate_cljs_test pins rf.story/register-substrate! as THE public form (the sub-namespace call is the internal one); and skills/re-frame2 names the same qualified spelling when explaining why story-mcp list-substrates is browser-only. It is also the WRITE half of the pair whose READ half (registered-substrates) sits on the facade -- keeping the reader and moving the writer is the split §Keep product facades coherent forbids."}
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "registered-substrates" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "The READ half of the substrate pair: the set of registered substrate ids, which is how a custom shell or a tooling caller enumerates what a variant's :substrates opt-in may legally name. A var-row of tools/story/spec/API.md §Registry queries and of docs/story/api/reference.md, so it is a documented facade surface and needs its manifest row -- a documented facade surface with no row is the exact accretion the facade audit exists to stop. Kept with register-substrate! as one workflow: register at boot, enumerate later."}
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "mount-shell!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch for a tooling product: the one call that puts Story on screen, and for most hosts the second line of their Story entry namespace after configure!. tools/story/spec/003-Render-Shell.md §Shell lifecycle is its normative home; tools/story/spec/API.md §Shell lifecycle and docs/story/api/reference.md row it; docs/story/index.md shows it in the host entry point and Tutorial-Playwright.md assumes it as the page-root mount. CLJS-only because mounting a Reagent tree at a DOM node has no JVM equivalent -- that reader conditional is why the JVM generator cannot see it, and says nothing about its status."}
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "unmount-shell!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "The teardown half of the mount pair Conventions §Lifecycle-verb law names, and idempotent by contract: what a host calls when it leaves Story mode, and what a test fixture calls between shell mounts. Rowed in tools/story/spec/API.md §Shell lifecycle. Splitting it from mount-shell! would split one two-call workflow across two requires."}
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "active-shell" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "The READ of the shell pair -- the live shell handle or nil. Load-bearing rather than cosmetic: 003-Render-Shell.md §Shell lifecycle makes v1 a singleton in which a second mount-shell! tears the first down, so a host or fixture that must not clobber an existing shell has to ask first, and this is the only way to ask. Rowed in tools/story/spec/API.md §Shell lifecycle and docs/story/api/reference.md."}

  ;; ---- re-frame.fresco — the CLJS ARM of a SPLIT-HOST door ----
  ;;
  ;; Unlike every block above, this namespace IS JVM-loadable: it sits in the
  ;; generator's `jvm-namespaces`, where `ns-publics` returns its `#?(:clj …)`
  ;; arm — the three authoring macros, rowed under `:classification` below.
  ;; What the JVM cannot see is the `#?(:cljs (do …))` arm: the runtime
  ;; aliases that exist only under ClojureScript. So the rows land HERE, for
  ;; the reason this key exists — a row the JVM generator cannot introspect —
  ;; and the CLJS probe reconciles them against the live analyzer surface.
  ;; This is the exact MIRROR of `:jvm-only-classification` below, which does
  ;; the same bookkeeping in the other direction for the same class of `.cljc`
  ;; door; between them a split-host namespace is inventoried on both hosts
  ;; and neither can silently gain or lose a public.
  ;;
  ;; `advanced` throughout, restrictive-by-default per spec/API.md §Tier
  ;; taxonomy: Fresco is an OPT-IN published substrate, so its door is not
  ;; the front-porch set the Guide and skills load by default (that set is
  ;; `re-frame.core`'s, which is what doc-guide-check projects against).
  ;; `:kind` is the curated human assertion the probe deliberately does NOT
  ;; reconcile (see `cljs-probe`'s "Why `:kind` is NOT reconciled"):
  ;; `error-boundary` and `portal` are value-`def`s bound to component objects
  ;; placed in hiccup rather than called, so they read `:var`; the rest are
  ;; `defn`s or explicit `fn`s. Owner splits on the contract that actually
  ;; owns each door. The root-lifecycle trio — `client-root`, `render!`,
  ;; `unmount!` — is 006's, whose §The client root states the MUST all four
  ;; React view adapters answer and whose rows for
  ;; `re-frame.adapter.reagent` and `re-frame.adapter.uix` carry the same
  ;; three names under the same owner. The frame-boundary
  ;; heads are 002's, and the rest are 006's, which also carries Fresco's
  ;; row in the canonical substrate inventory.
  {:namespace "re-frame.fresco" :var "as-component"   :kind :fn  :facade? false :tier :advanced :owner "006"  :status "v1" :runtime-verified? true}
